premises.content:
A computational algorithm that computes Z-valued topological invariants of inhomogeneous noninteracting electronic systems by (i) approximating the spectral projector $ \hat{P}=	heta(E_{F}-\hat{H}) $ via the kernel polynomial method (KPM) using a polynomial expansion with Jackson kernel up to order $M$, (ii) constructing a local topological marker operator $ \hat{
u} $ which is a polynomial in $ \hat{P} $ and sparse symmetry/position operators, and (iii) estimating the trace-per-volume of $ \hat{
u} $ by the stochastic trace approximation with $R$ random phase vectors localized in a subsystem $S$. Concretely, the method uses the KPM expansion $ \hat{P}(\varepsilon,H)\approx\sum_{m=0}^{M}\mu_{m}(\varepsilon)T_{m}(H) $ (with moments $ \mu_{m} $ and Chebyshev polynomials $ T_{m} $) to evaluate $ \hat{
u} $ acting on vectors by repeated sparse matrix-vector multiplications, and then evaluates $ \mathrm{Tr}_{S}(\hat{
u})\approx(1/(R|S|))\sum_{i=1}^{R}\langle r_{i}|\hat{
u}|r_{i}angle $ with a small number $R$ of random vectors $|r_{i}angle$ localized in $S$. The algorithm requires only sparse matrix storage and repeated application of $ \hat{H} $ to vectors (no eigenvalue solvers), and is implemented with the Jackson kernel KPM recursion for numerical stability and controlled polynomial approximation of the projector.
